#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f020b27f-1cd4-550b-a83a-2afbb7e5db72" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimension of the Null Space of Matrices Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb1c773-5efa-5640-802f-7172570b982c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317665} {:answer/id #uuid "37e6251c-c663-504a-9ae0-4e99abf04d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317744} {:answer/id #uuid "63d7c35a-f384-50a4-9462-b80fd457652e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317906} {:answer/id #uuid "d39ad45e-1f94-51d7-a97b-6fce87394432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318268} {:answer/id #uuid "da8784b4-eb6d-52d4-a21f-b740a1c6ea6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d39ad45e-1f94-51d7-a97b-6fce87394432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318268} :answer-field/id #uuid "7745095e-0993-59ff-9359-1232381545d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b1d1e88-f02a-5243-9bdc-9d244b4db578" :question/math-academy-id "q-34902" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.$\n- If $B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=2.$\n- If $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The matrix $A$ has $1$ pivot column and $1$ non-pivot column. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f020b27f-1cd4-550b-a83a-2afbb7e5db72" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimension of the Null Space of Matrices Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285ed021-2a5b-563c-9cb6-a55ef00e5f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317691} {:answer/id #uuid "7e27ee27-ef83-527f-ad6e-6ed1940373d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317995} {:answer/id #uuid "882c9335-98b9-51dd-8014-126aafeeb87b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318026} {:answer/id #uuid "982ad340-ed13-552d-bca5-2129fbb6d423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318085} {:answer/id #uuid "f19ae1c4-9c02-5103-bc45-ec9e3b3e8123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "285ed021-2a5b-563c-9cb6-a55ef00e5f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317691} :answer-field/id #uuid "49038ece-c8a5-5724-aab1-227760f5017a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56ebc46d-299b-5fda-a402-a93bacc04806" :question/math-academy-id "q-34973" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.$\n\nII. If $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=3.$\n\nIII. If $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The matrix $A$ has $3$ pivot columns and $2$ non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $B$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c78bfa2-14dd-582b-852b-ca7700249537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321491} {:answer/id #uuid "301e783a-5111-5681-8b24-c0b31cc6b5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321516} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d62ed0-9790-5c2d-af76-f2b03f3b7fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322472} {:answer/id #uuid "cb454d8c-6d6c-5aa5-8bc0-5dd114da5a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322727} {:answer/id #uuid "d879594f-5354-5863-8eee-b752be0672b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8d62ed0-9790-5c2d-af76-f2b03f3b7fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322472} :answer-field/id #uuid "814d813a-dbf0-5b90-a6cd-f593a61a455e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9288acd6-ddf2-55d8-ba92-e1c27cfdfe7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35425" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$  is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\n- $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55d0294c-efb3-587e-905a-3c1b8e2615e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321826} {:answer/id #uuid "65f0fcbf-a291-5708-a596-bef3bcdab317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321937} {:answer/id #uuid "d6939f50-a62f-5c42-ba4a-3fc659ffa0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322823} {:answer/id #uuid "e92f675e-e3b4-5bf3-a460-64ebffc64401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322975} {:answer/id #uuid "ec305288-e455-5047-aff1-0f1c99fd0dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6939f50-a62f-5c42-ba4a-3fc659ffa0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322823} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e3a4bf3-4101-5c9a-b2b3-613ebd7f6042" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b252a44d-8417-5bdc-8a9b-ad6e81276b7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36075" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=0$ has the unique  solution $⟨0,0⟩$\n- $⟨5,-4⟩$ is a solution to $Ax=0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. Again,  according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71b251ee-d946-5721-a2b9-1693d09c5c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322039} {:answer/id #uuid "7da470d2-ccbc-5645-a60f-2f6ebe57efbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322136} {:answer/id #uuid "85d5fec7-973d-579d-a255-e54fca9e6d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322208} {:answer/id #uuid "90af79c9-12f5-51a4-ad66-cc6a98adbadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322295} {:answer/id #uuid "b07629d0-8dd6-59bb-8924-bd0ada783bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da470d2-ccbc-5645-a60f-2f6ebe57efbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322136} :answer-field/id #uuid "41c8417b-9a7e-5c89-bd0e-7828c61e149e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac28aad1-833b-50ff-aaf3-b0e247f97248" :question/math-academy-id "q-37260" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N:3\\le x<9}$ and $B={x\\in N:3x<10}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $∅⊆A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all natural numbers greater than or equal to $3$ but less than $9.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={3,4,5,6,7,8}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers which satisfy the inequality $3x<10.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $4\\in A$ but $4\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $1\\in B$ but $1\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆A$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36db4a98-c77f-566f-add8-d784e34a8201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321562} {:answer/id #uuid "5df5cc11-0f0f-584e-96ba-cffd0337f572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321890} {:answer/id #uuid "85a2068a-1696-56a5-b712-f62da3e0154b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322204} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd0c8eb-c194-53d9-8e6c-b4c7a5ab237b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322602} {:answer/id #uuid "ebea7076-7b67-5659-a831-af59491bded5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5df5cc11-0f0f-584e-96ba-cffd0337f572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321890} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d158276-bf8f-5b2c-82c4-2a317bf74e56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fab40d0-11c4-5889-9e14-2d675fe9ad31" :question/math-academy-id "q-37261" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={2{n}^{2}∣n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 3}$ and $B={2,0,8,18}?$ \n\n- $A⊈B$\n- $∅⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $A.$ Substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3}$ into the formula $2{n}^{2},$ we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,2\\cdot {1}^{2}=2 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,2\\cdot {2}^{2}=8 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,2\\cdot {3}^{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA={2,8,18}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊈B$ is false. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $0\\in B$ but $0\\notin A.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "370f10a6-a504-50e7-885b-2671f68902f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321565} {:answer/id #uuid "404a88c2-2623-5a2a-850a-94a9a7f3f8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321656} {:answer/id #uuid "49ada476-ffca-550c-a179-ed71c8e76eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321726} {:answer/id #uuid "8eacfea1-1473-5d55-889f-a805fae453c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322280} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6626e6-2729-5047-931a-4bfc158b9452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49ada476-ffca-550c-a179-ed71c8e76eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321726} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b39bcc3-1fcf-5175-a29d-38fc05faf96a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac9d3f90-ae5f-56ba-9210-f0c988989a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-37267" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={5,10,15,20,25,30,35,…}$ and $B={5n∣n\\in N,3\\le n\\le 7}?$\n\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊆B$\n- $A⊈B$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $B.$ Substituting the values $n\\in {3,4,5,6,7}$ into the formula $5n,$ we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=3 & \\,⇒\\,5(3)=15 \\\\ n=4 & \\,⇒\\,5(4)=20 \\\\ n=5 & \\,⇒\\,5(5)=25 \\\\ n=6 & \\,⇒\\,5(6)=30 \\\\ n=7 & \\,⇒\\,5(7)=35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nB={15,20,25,30,35}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad92e40-a623-50ab-87e7-4252481257f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321355} {:answer/id #uuid "64178fd7-865d-58f5-9a13-832c56ef5564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321924} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb35b97-84e7-5df4-a7a3-927674c811be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322751} {:answer/id #uuid "d704a155-46ac-57c6-84be-3b36751485d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322825} {:answer/id #uuid "f36a2d75-c29c-54f8-8082-64ac91de7169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64178fd7-865d-58f5-9a13-832c56ef5564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321924} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ca7348b-8edc-5c13-a183-d7eac2e7e33e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14dd51d6-4da8-56bc-8036-6e67390713d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37449" :question/problem "The function   $f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(4,8).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(4,8)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)>0$\n- The point $(4,8)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4x-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y)=-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2y)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+4x & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(4,8),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4,8)=[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(4,8),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(4,8)=det({f}^{″}(4,8))=(-32)\\cdot (-2)-{4}^{2}=48>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)=({f}^{″}(4,8))_{11}=-32<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(4,8)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)<0,$ the point $(4,8)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4110eaf9-0cb0-5969-b28c-2a303e2041ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324497} {:answer/id #uuid "56595fa9-4eaa-5a74-ae32-68f2ac4645c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324690} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1dfd97-9132-59f6-9626-a6cc2b0ff6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325178} {:answer/id #uuid "96b5a3e2-b8c9-5048-9cf0-cb845c205afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325256} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c001c3-5a17-5270-a705-71299a7e3dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96b5a3e2-b8c9-5048-9cf0-cb845c205afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325256} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fe2826b-f01a-5b14-8b24-64c33e485d40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e6f3463-c123-5a14-aa68-90e5211244ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-38905" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is a bijection? \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=6$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31028d3e-3da3-5e91-ba93-883106de5e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324363} {:answer/id #uuid "7c996f4a-0a3f-58bb-b379-d74b2c11563d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325020} {:answer/id #uuid "80e4f3b0-76c6-563e-b3b3-10b6cf13085c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325048} {:answer/id #uuid "a30ac905-cfe8-5e57-ad4c-84f77a90b748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325355} {:answer/id #uuid "d78bdcfe-4922-587f-923c-7eb1435a4fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e4f3b0-76c6-563e-b3b3-10b6cf13085c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325048} :answer-field/id #uuid "6accfeb7-721b-58cf-8967-701b70ef39fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d76ba203-8385-5ab0-a588-ad7d93b64899" :question/math-academy-id "q-38907" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)?$\n\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=3$\n- $T$ is invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ we must have that $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5,$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd41dde-5f28-5a65-b5bc-957bff0e15d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324354} {:answer/id #uuid "3326d6f6-511c-5dd7-959a-2137034a6f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324380} {:answer/id #uuid "35e8cc1a-fd6a-50d1-a664-8a939f061cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324411} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf8dd4f-5323-5609-a163-cedf96c6b875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325162} {:answer/id #uuid "b38a5b32-3c84-51e8-a172-ae1aaa23fa96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3326d6f6-511c-5dd7-959a-2137034a6f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324380} :answer-field/id #uuid "baa633e0-889f-5434-a142-3f41f1b5c31c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3221319-81c0-55a5-8884-351b35c9f34f" :question/math-academy-id "q-40372" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$\n- $[T]_{B}=[T]_{C}^{-1}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/9b/9ba3d1f0eebdfc82c40f36bdbe16896ca15516b623c695b72c331861955329bc.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c7b53a-701a-51a2-a16d-4e21a8a4cc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327157} {:answer/id #uuid "52c48599-aa51-58b9-b7f4-01369eca46de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327652} {:answer/id #uuid "90f50a2a-111f-54d0-a288-d415a537c42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328199} {:answer/id #uuid "c36d0ce7-0114-5581-9387-0b4151c0500e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328642} {:answer/id #uuid "ec160af8-f144-50c1-b2ac-330cdc7c76a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c36d0ce7-0114-5581-9387-0b4151c0500e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328642} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f303ed6-16a7-554a-b9b7-791202045199" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "005a0156-3d99-59d1-ab99-adf9b9b4c7b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-42264" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $AB=BA$\n\nII. $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n\nIII. $A(B-C)=(B-C)A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $A(B-C)\\ne (B-C)A$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59e0d9fc-b17e-5366-9b3a-2c1c5e9642a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327723} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6e3679-0338-5a9b-b1c0-c22989f97c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328185} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7d5c47-635e-59a4-a4cb-ae2b2fbd7be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328311} {:answer/id #uuid "b61a2feb-e5b1-58eb-94c3-56fd1cf86ef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328518} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3bb7c8-6b61-5a32-ad7a-c3544ff78a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f6e3679-0338-5a9b-b1c0-c22989f97c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328185} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf37b99f-cb96-521c-a7b3-8d065cf5ff49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cea2f24d-948b-5d70-91c6-23df57186795" :question/math-academy-id "q-42267" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $5\\times 5$ matrices, and  ${I}_{5}$ is the $5\\times 5$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=BA$\n- $A(B+C)=AB+AC$\n- $B{I}_{5}={I}_{5}B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $B{I}_{5}={I}_{5}B=B.$\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b0a0de0-f401-550d-bc7b-dc018e343ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327168} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa2b61a-17ee-53bf-8abf-069bde2204e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327307} {:answer/id #uuid "5deed627-985e-576b-84e2-731ea2f85f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327760} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a7897c-8483-5439-a17c-d60aafc7c976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328825} {:answer/id #uuid "dd036c3d-369c-5bca-92d6-3c4440fa2ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd036c3d-369c-5bca-92d6-3c4440fa2ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328865} :answer-field/id #uuid "9171d90d-c0fb-5285-965c-0713a220ab28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e92499c-048f-53fc-971b-21b3cc496d2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42375" :question/problem "$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $J,K,$ and $L?$\n\n- The product $JK$ is well defined\n- The product $KL$ is well defined\n- The product $JL$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $J$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, $K$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, and $L$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nThere are $4$ columns in $J$ and $3$ rows in $K.$ Since the number of columns in $J$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $K,$ the matrices $J$ and $K$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{J}_{3\\times 4}{\\times }_{\\ne }{K}_{3\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $K$ and $2$ rows in $L.$ Since the number of columns in $K$ equals the number of rows in $L$, the matrices $K$ and $L$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{K}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{L}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $4$ columns in $J$ and $2$ rows in $L.$ Since the number of columns in $J$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $L$, the matrices $J$ and $L$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{J}_{3\\times 4}{\\times }_{\\ne }{L}_{2\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement II is true, while statements I and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ad6981a-9523-5a55-b446-ad602d60f251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327989} {:answer/id #uuid "8e824145-4d04-581e-9945-43cd36d69460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328173} {:answer/id #uuid "af30d2d3-879a-5a7c-9135-76adf58a61ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328452} {:answer/id #uuid "afac5d85-693a-53a0-ba73-ace5dfab1fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328455} {:answer/id #uuid "bccb4986-b752-5e1b-90b1-ca5d58b9dd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bccb4986-b752-5e1b-90b1-ca5d58b9dd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328575} :answer-field/id #uuid "21644d86-c7e8-5190-a2ff-a13362c0fdcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca108716-0018-5080-8610-49601a0656af" :question/math-academy-id "q-42376" :question/problem "$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $D,E,$ and $F?$\n\n- The product $ED$ is well defined\n- The product $FD$ is well defined\n- The product $EF$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $E$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, and $F$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $E$ and $2$ rows in $D.$ Since the number of columns in $E$ equals the number of rows in $D,$ the matrices $E$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{E}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{D}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $F$ and $2$ rows in $D.$ Since the number of columns in $F$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $D,$ the matrices $F$ and $D$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{F}_{2\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{D}_{2\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $2$ columns in $E$ and $2$ rows in $F.$ Since the number of columns in $E$ equals the number of rows in $F,$ the matrices $E$ and $F$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{E}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{F}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02795ba8-eecf-5b89-ac37-f43fca3a4edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326962} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab1ecd4-5959-5f6b-9f75-c0e651356239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328126} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7ecf7a-538b-525f-9ffa-93a26130c0fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328741} {:answer/id #uuid "db87c366-6f5f-55c8-a7e7-b7324162778b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328852} {:answer/id #uuid "db8cbe79-3588-5411-992b-a8c8ec7dc1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db87c366-6f5f-55c8-a7e7-b7324162778b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328852} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7f3cfd9-9933-55d5-9def-e04d4918c771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e41c04ee-99fe-56c1-8773-ea0949cc66d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-42377" :question/problem "$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $L,M,$ and $N?$\n\n- The product $ML$ is well defined\n- The product $MN$ is well defined\n- The product $LN$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $L$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, $M$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $N$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere are $3$ columns in $M$ and $2$ rows in $L.$ Since the number of columns in $M$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $L,$ the matrices $M$ and $L$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{M}_{2\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{L}_{2\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $M$ and $3$ rows in $N.$ Since the number of columns in $M$ equals the number of rows in $N,$ the matrices $M$ and $N$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{M}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{N}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $L$ and $3$ rows in $N.$ Since the number of columns in $L$ equals the number of rows in $N,$ the matrices $L$ and $N$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{L}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{N}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false."}]}
